:"text","text":"## Refactoring Applied\n\n**Naming evaluation**\n- Current helper name `cellKey` did not clearly distinguish the key-generation operation from a key value.\n- Renamed it to `toCellKey`.\n- Renamed the destructured map entry variable from `cellKey` to `key`, avoiding shadowing/confusion with the helper.\n\n**Change**\n- `src/game-of-life.ts`: `cellKey` → `toCellKey`; corresponding call sites updated.\n\n**Simple Design assessment**\n1. **Tests pass:** unable to verify because `pnpm test` is blocked: `vitest: not found`.\n2. **Reveals intent:** improved helper name to express conversion explicitly.\n3. **No duplication:** no clear duplication suitable for extraction without reducing clarity.\n4. **Fewest elements:** preserved existing algorithm and structure; no additional elements introduced.\n\n**APP mass**\n- Before: **98**\n- After: **98**\n- Change: **0**\n\nThe refactoring is intentionally mass-neutral: Rule 2 (revealing intent) takes precedence over APP reduction.\n\n**Verification**\n- `pnpm test` attempted before and after the refactor.\n- Result: blocked by missing Vitest executable (`sh: 1: vitest: not found`).\n- Type checking could not be reached because the chained verification command stopped at the unavailable test executable."}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_07-07-49_game-of-life-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_gpt-5-6-terra/.pi/agents","results":[{"agent":"refactor","agentSource":"project","task":"Test file: src/game-of-life.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/game-of-life.ts\nPassing tests: unverified (pnpm test blocked: vitest executable missing)\nRecent Green phase: no code change; adjacent underpopulation was already represented by the implementation at that stage.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. Run autonomously, return when done.","exitCode":0,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task: Test file: src/game-of-life.spec.ts\nImplementation file: src/game-of-life.ts\nPassing tests: unverified (pnpm test blocked: vitest executable missing)\nRecent Green phase: no code change; adjacent underpopulation was already represented by the implementation at that stage.\n\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green. Run autonomously, return when done."}],"timestamp":1784963430013},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_YDDb6mOUduMXNQPJI2eQvRFk","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/game-of-life.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_epLtpsIt9HNPBuJudL8mAwVP","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/game-of-life.spec.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7C04HgqeOcZNu7G2hALiNuOR","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"package.json"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_zPa418XDObfpJtfFT5tDTIhO","name":"find","arguments":{"path":".","pattern":"AGENTS.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"azure/gpt-5.6-terra@swedencentral","usage":{"input":420,"output":127,"cacheRead":5760,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":33,"totalTokens":6307,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784963430032,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-975dc02a-3989-4d79-a44b-edb37dc19663","responseModel":"gpt-5.6-terra-2026-07-09"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_YDDb6mOUduMXNQPJI2eQvRFk","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"export type Cell = [number, number];\n\nconst cellKey = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;\n\nexport function nextGeneration(liveCells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const liveCellKeys = new Set(liveCells.map(([x, y]) => cellKey(x, y)));\n  const neighbourCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of liveCells) {\n    for (let deltaX = -1; deltaX <= 1; deltaX += 1) {\n      for (let deltaY = -1; deltaY <= 1; deltaY += 1) {\n        if (deltaX === 0 && deltaY === 0) continue;\n        const neighborKey = cellKey(x + deltaX, y + deltaY);\n        neighbourCounts.set(neighborKey, (neighbourCounts.get(neighborKey) ?? 0) + 1);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return [...neighbourCounts].flatMap(([cellKey, count]) => {\n    const [x, y] = cellKey.split(\",\").map(Number);\n    return count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveCellKeys.has(cellKey)) ? [[x, y] as Cell] : [];\n  });\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784963431643},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_epLtpsIt9HNPBuJudL8mAwVP","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"import { describe, expect, it } from \"vitest\";\nimport { nextGeneration } from \"./game-of-life.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"Game of Life nextGeneration\", () => {\n  it(\"returns [] for an empty generation -- []\", () => {\n    expect(nextGeneration([])).toEqual([]);\n  });\n  it(\"single cell dies from underpopulation -- [(0, 0)] becomes []\", () => {\n    expect(nextGeneration([[0, 0]])).toEqual([]);\n  });\n  it(\"two adjacent live cells die from underpopulation -- [(0, 1), (1, 1)] becomes []\", () => {\n    expect(nextGeneration([[0, 1], [1, 1]])).toEqual([]);\n  });\n  it(\"live cells with two or three neighbours survive -- the center (1, 1) remains alive\", () => {\n    expect(nextGeneration([[1, 1], [0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 1]])).toContainEqual([1, 1]);\n  });\n  it.todo(\"an overcrowded live center dies -- Rule 3 example becomes [(0, 2), (2, 2), (0, 1), (2, 1), (0, 0), (2, 0)]\");\n  it.todo(\"a dead cell with exactly three neighbours is born -- [(0, 1), (1, 1), (0, 0)] becomes [(0, 1), (1, 1), (0, 0), (1, 0)]\");\n  it.todo(\"a blinker alternates vertically to horizontally and back -- Gen 1 [(-1, 1), (0, 1), (1, 1)], Gen 2 [(0, 0), (0, 1), (0, 2)]\");\n  it.todo(\"a block still life remains unchanged -- [(0, 0), (1, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1)]\");\n});\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784963431643},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7C04HgqeOcZNu7G2hALiNuOR","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"name\": \"tdd-experiment-run\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"test\": \"vitest run\",\n    \"test:unit:basic\": \"vitest run\",\n    \"test:watch\": \"vitest\",\n    \"test:coverage\": \"vitest run --coverage --coverage.reporter=json-summary\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"typescript\": \"^5.3.0\",\n    \"tsx\": \"^4.7.0\",\n    \"vitest\": \"^1.0.0\",\n    \"@vitest/coverage-v8\": \"^1.0.0\",\n    \"eslint\": \"^9.0.0\",\n    \"eslint-plugin-sonarjs\": \"^1.0.0\",\n    \"typescript-eslint\": \"^8.0.0\"\n  },\n  \"pnpm\": {\n    \"onlyBuiltDependencies\": [\"esbuild\"]\n  }\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784963431643},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_zPa418XDObfpJtfFT5tDTIhO","toolName":"find","content":[{"type":"text","text":".pi/AGENTS.md\nAGENTS.md"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784963431644},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_CCSvYjoksQ2Ex9xIQbKjLiZb","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"AGENTS.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_NAl3ymNFHHPtbMt56Bra7cBS","name":"read","arguments":{"path":".pi/AGENTS.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_G9vNoi0sd1klGTUCHyN2coUL","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test","timeout":120}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"azure/gpt-5.6-terra@swedencentral","usage":{"input":7229,"output":231,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":152,"totalTokens":7460,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784963431644,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-a65a52c7-fa3b-456b-87e6-aa8dddf6d088","responseModel":"gpt-5.6-terra-2026-07-09"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_CCSvYjoksQ2Ex9xIQbKjLiZb","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Test-Driven Development (TDD) Rules -- Hybrid (v6.2.1, pi)\n\n## CRITICAL: One Continuous Autonomous Run -- Never Stop at a Phase Boundary\n\nThis entire workflow -- Test List, then Red/Green/Refactor for every test,\nthrough to writing `experiment-done.txt` -- is **one single autonomous\nturn**. There are no human-approval gates and no natural stopping points\nbetween phases.\n\nA phase-completion line (e.g. \"Test List Phase Complete\", \"Red Phase\nComplete\", a returned refactor subagent) is a **checkpoint, not a\nterminus**. After emitting it you MUST immediately continue with the next\nphase's action in the same turn:\n\n- After the **Test List** -> read `red/SKILL.md` and produce `## Red` for test 1.\n- After **Red** -> read `green/SKILL.md` and produce `## Green`.\n- After **Green** -> invoke the `refactor` subagent.\n- After **Refactor returns** -> produce `## Red` for the next test.\n\nThe only place your turn may end is **after** you have written\n`experiment-done.txt` containing `DONE`. Ending the turn on a \"Proceeding\nto...\" / \"Next Step...\" announcement -- without actually taking that step\n-- is the single most common failure mode on this harness and invalidates\nthe run. Announcing an action is not doing it; always do it in the same turn.\n\n## CRITICAL: Mandatory Output Format\n\nThis workflow runs on **pi**, where skills are auto-loaded documents, not tool calls.\nThe model reads each SKILL.md once and then follows its instructions directly.\nThis means the measurement pipeline **cannot count tool invocations** to track cycles.\nInstead, it relies on **text markers** in the assistant output.\n\n### Phase-Completion Markers Are MANDATORY\n\nEvery TDD phase MUST produce a specific text marker in your output.\nThese markers are mechanically parsed to compute `cycle_count`, `predictions_correct_rate`,\nand `refactorings_applied`. Missing markers silently zero the corresponding metric,\ninvalidating the data point.\n\n| Phase     | Mandatory Output Marker                   | What the Parser Counts               |\n|-----------|-------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| Test List | `## Test List` heading                    | test-list phase occurrence           |\n| Red       | `## Red` heading                          | red-phase cycle (`cycle_count`)      |\n| Red       | `Red Phase Complete:` + prediction lines  | `predictions_correct`, `predictions_total` |\n| Green     | `## Green` heading                        | green-phase occurrence               |\n| Refactor  | `subagent` tool call with `agent: \"refactor\"` | `refactorings_applied`          |\n\n**Format for each Red phase output:**\n\n```\n## Red -- Test N: <test description>\n\n<prediction and failure verification steps>\n\nRed Phase Complete:\n**Compilation Prediction**: <what you expect> Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: <what you expect> Correct\n\n<Result summary>\n```\n\nThe two prediction lines (`Compilation Prediction: ... Correct` / `Runtime Prediction: ... Correct`)\nare **parsed mechanically**. You MUST output them verbatim with `Correct` or `Incorrect` at the end.\nDo not abbreviate, summarize, or collapse them. Do not skip them when a test already passes --\nin that case, write:\n\n```\n## Red -- Test N: <test description>\n\nTest already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\n```\n\n**Format for Green phase output:**\n\n```\n## Green -- <brief summary of what was added>\n```\n\n**Format for Test List phase output:**\n\n```\n## Test List\n\n<test list details>\n```\n\n## Overview\n\nThis project follows strict Test-Driven Development practices using the Red-Green-Refactor cycle.\nv6 keeps red and green in a shared context so the predictions, error messages, and minimal\nimplementations stay coherent -- and isolates refactoring so the model evaluates the resulting\ncode on its own merits.\n\n## Architecture: Skills + Subagent\n\n- **`test-list`, `red`, `green`** -- Their SKILL.md files are auto-loaded as context.\n  Read each SKILL.md **before the first use** of that phase, then follow its instructions\n  directly in the main context. You execute the phase yourself, following the skill content.\n- **Refactor** -- Runs as a **Subagent with isolated context** via the `subagent` tool.\n  The refactor agent sees only the current source/tests, not the full red/green history.\n  Hypothesis: refactoring benefits most from a fresh perspective free of implementation bias.\n\nDo NOT skip reading the skill files. Do NOT skip the mandatory output markers.\n\n### Which Mechanism to Use:\n\n| Phase         | Mechanism                              | How to Execute                                              |\n|---------------|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Test List     | **Skill document** (main context)      | Read `.pi/skills/test-list/SKILL.md`, follow its steps      |\n| Red Phase     | **Skill document** (main context)      | Read `.pi/skills/red/SKILL.md`, follow its steps           |\n| Green Phase   | **Skill document** (main context)      | Read `.pi/skills/green/SKILL.md`, follow its steps         |\n| Refactor Phase| **Subagent** (isolated context)        | `subagent` tool with `agent: \"refactor\"`, `agentScope: \"both\"` |\n\n## TDD Workflow\n\n### 1. Test List Phase\n**READ SKILL**: `.pi/skills/test-list/SKILL.md`\n\nProvide: feature, test file path, implementation file path, requirements.\n\nThe skill creates a comprehensive test list using `it.todo()` covering every rule and example from the specification.\n\n**DO NOT** write the test list yourself. Follow the skill instructions and produce the `## Test List` marker.\n\n**DO NOT** end your turn after the test list. The moment the `## Test List`\nmarker is complete, continue in the same turn into the Red Phase for test 1\n(read `red/SKILL.md`, produce `## Red`). See \"One Continuous Autonomous Run\"\nat the top -- the Test-List -> Red boundary is where runs most often stall.\n\n### 2. Red Phase\n**READ SKILL**: `.pi/skills/red/SKILL.md`\n\nProvide: test file path, which `it.todo()` to activate, current passing-test count, implementation file path.\n\nThe skill activates exactly ONE test, makes explicit predictions, and verifies failure.\n\n**DO NOT** write test code yourself. Follow the skill instructions and produce the `## Red` and `Red Phase Complete:` markers with prediction lines.\n\n### 3. Green Phase\n**READ SKILL**: `.pi/skills/green/SKILL.md`\n\nProvide: test file path, failing test name, current error, implementation file path.\n\nThe skill implements minimal code to make the test pass -- hardcoded returns are fine for early tests.\n\n**DO NOT** write implementation code yourself. Follow the skill instructions and produce the `## Green` marker.\n\n### 4. Refactor Phase\n**INVOKE SUBAGENT**: Use the `subagent` tool with these parameters:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"agent\": \"refactor\",\n  \"agentScope\": \"both\",\n  \"task\": \"Test file: src/<feature>.spec.ts\\nImplementation file: src/<feature>.ts\\nPassing tests: <count>\\nRecent Green phase: <one-line summary of what was just added>\\n\\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\\n\\nRun autonomously, return when done.\"\n}\n```\n\n`agentScope: \"both\"` is REQUIRED because the `refactor` agent lives in `.pi/agents/` (project-local). Without this flag the subagent extension only sees user-level agents and the call fails with \"Unknown agent: refactor\".\n\nThe agent will improve code while keeping tests green:\n- MUST attempt at least one refactoring (or document why none is possible)\n- Evaluate naming FIRST\n- Apply Four Rules of Simple Design (priority order)\n- Calculate APP (Absolute Priority Premise) mass before/after\n\n**DO NOT** refactor code yourself -- let the subagent do it. After it returns, read its summary, apply any test-runs needed for sanity, and proceed to the next Red phase.\n\n### 5. Repeat\nReturn to step 2 (Red phase) for the next test.\n\n## Core TDD Principles\n\n### TDD Mindset\nTDD practices will feel counterintuitive:\n- **Hardcoded returns feel \"too simple\"** -- This is correct!\n- **The urge to implement ahead is strong** -- Resist this\n- **Minimal steps feel inefficient** -- They actually accelerate development\n- **Predictions feel unnecessary** -- They build crucial understanding\n\n### Common TDD Failure Modes\n- **MISSING OUTPUT MARKERS** -- The most critical failure mode on pi.\n  Every cycle MUST have `## Red` and `## Green` headings.\n  Every Red cycle with a failing test MUST have `Red Phase Complete:` with prediction lines.\n- Multiple active tests at once\n- Implementing beyond what tests demand\n- Skipping predictions\n- Avoiding refactoring\n- Refactoring in the main context instead of via the subagent\n\n## Technical Setup: TypeScript and Vitest\n\n### TypeScript Conventions\n- Use proper type annotations\n- Leverage TypeScript's type checking during development\n- Name files after their content\n- Import with explicit `.js` extensions for local modules\n\n### Test File Conventions\n- Use `.spec.ts` extension for test files\n- Place tests near implementation files\n- Import test functions: `import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\"`\n\n### Running Tests\n\nRun tests with `pnpm test`.\n\n### Example Test Template\n\nThe test list comes from the kata's specification -- do not add generic \"validate input types\" or \"edge cases\" tests unless the spec calls for them.\n\n```typescript\n// some-feature.spec.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { someFeature } from \"./some-feature.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"Some Feature\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"[first behaviour from the spec]\");\n  it.todo(\"[second behaviour from the spec]\");\n});\n```\n\n## TDD Autonomous Execution\n\nThe TDD cycle runs autonomously -- no human-approval gates between phases.\nThe measurement pipeline parses the output text for phase markers\nand subagent tool calls; user prompts inserted between phases would\nsplit that sequence and produce unattributable cycles.\n\nWhen executing:\n- Do NOT wait for human approval between phases\n- Complete the full TDD cycle without interruption\n\n### Autonomous Workflow\n\n1. **Test List Phase** -- Read skill, produce `## Test List` marker\n2. **For each test:**\n   - **Red Phase** -- Read skill, produce `## Red` and `Red Phase Complete:` with predictions\n   - **Green Phase** -- Read skill, produce `## Green` marker\n   - **Refactor Phase** -- Invoke `subagent` tool with `agent: \"refactor\"`, `agentScope: \"both\"`\n3. **Continue** until all tests are implemented\n\n### Required Task Content for the Refactor Subagent\n\nThe refactor subagent has no memory of the red/green phases. Pass everything it needs in the `task` parameter:\n\n```\nTest file: [path]\nImplementation file: [path]\nPassing tests: [count]\nRecent changes: [one-line summary of the Green phase]\n\nRun autonomously, return after completion.\n```\n\nAfter the subagent returns, read its summary and proceed directly to the next Red phase.\n\n### Done Marker\n\nWhen all tests are implemented and passing, write a file `experiment-done.txt` with the single word `DONE` as its only content. Do not write any other summary or report file.\n\n## Remember\n\n- **NEVER STOP AT A PHASE BOUNDARY** -- Test List -> Red -> Green -> Refactor -> next Red all happen in one turn. Announcing the next phase is not doing it; take the action in the same turn. The only turn-end is after `experiment-done.txt` says `DONE`.\n- **ALWAYS PRODUCE THE MANDATORY MARKERS** -- `## Red`, `Red Phase Complete:`, `## Green`, `## Test List`\n- **ALWAYS USE THE SUBAGENT** for refactor -- `subagent` tool with `agent: \"refactor\"`, `agentScope: \"both\"`\n- Follow skill content (test-list/red/green SKILL.md) for the actual work\n- Pass `agentScope: \"both\"` to the `subagent` tool so the project-local refactor agent is discovered\n- Trust the process -- discomfort is a signal you're doing it right\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784963434636},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_NAl3ymNFHHPtbMt56Bra7cBS","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Test-Driven Development (TDD) Rules -- Hybrid (v6.2.1, pi)\n\n## CRITICAL: One Continuous Autonomous Run -- Never Stop at a Phase Boundary\n\nThis entire workflow -- Test List, then Red/Green/Refactor for every test,\nthrough to writing `experiment-done.txt` -- is **one single autonomous\nturn**. There are no human-approval gates and no natural stopping points\nbetween phases.\n\nA phase-completion line (e.g. \"Test List Phase Complete\", \"Red Phase\nComplete\", a returned refactor subagent) is a **checkpoint, not a\nterminus**. After emitting it you MUST immediately continue with the next\nphase's action in the same turn:\n\n- After the **Test List** -> read `red/SKILL.md` and produce `## Red` for test 1.\n- After **Red** -> read `green/SKILL.md` and produce `## Green`.\n- After **Green** -> invoke the `refactor` subagent.\n- After **Refactor returns** -> produce `## Red` for the next test.\n\nThe only place your turn may end is **after** you have written\n`experiment-done.txt` containing `DONE`. Ending the turn on a \"Proceeding\nto...\" / \"Next Step...\" announcement -- without actually taking that step\n-- is the single most common failure mode on this harness and invalidates\nthe run. Announcing an action is not doing it; always do it in the same turn.\n\n## CRITICAL: Mandatory Output Format\n\nThis workflow runs on **pi**, where skills are auto-loaded documents, not tool calls.\nThe model reads each SKILL.md once and then follows its instructions directly.\nThis means the measurement pipeline **cannot count tool invocations** to track cycles.\nInstead, it relies on **text markers** in the assistant output.\n\n### Phase-Completion Markers Are MANDATORY\n\nEvery TDD phase MUST produce a specific text marker in your output.\nThese markers are mechanically parsed to compute `cycle_count`, `predictions_correct_rate`,\nand `refactorings_applied`. Missing markers silently zero the corresponding metric,\ninvalidating the data point.\n\n| Phase     | Mandatory Output Marker                   | What the Parser Counts               |\n|-----------|-------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| Test List | `## Test List` heading                    | test-list phase occurrence           |\n| Red       | `## Red` heading                          | red-phase cycle (`cycle_count`)      |\n| Red       | `Red Phase Complete:` + prediction lines  | `predictions_correct`, `predictions_total` |\n| Green     | `## Green` heading                        | green-phase occurrence               |\n| Refactor  | `subagent` tool call with `agent: \"refactor\"` | `refactorings_applied`          |\n\n**Format for each Red phase output:**\n\n```\n## Red -- Test N: <test description>\n\n<prediction and failure verification steps>\n\nRed Phase Complete:\n**Compilation Prediction**: <what you expect> Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: <what you expect> Correct\n\n<Result summary>\n```\n\nThe two prediction lines (`Compilation Prediction: ... Correct` / `Runtime Prediction: ... Correct`)\nare **parsed mechanically**. You MUST output them verbatim with `Correct` or `Incorrect` at the end.\nDo not abbreviate, summarize, or collapse them. Do not skip them when a test already passes --\nin that case, write:\n\n```\n## Red -- Test N: <test description>\n\nTest already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor.\n```\n\n**Format for Green phase output:**\n\n```\n## Green -- <brief summary of what was added>\n```\n\n**Format for Test List phase output:**\n\n```\n## Test List\n\n<test list details>\n```\n\n## Overview\n\nThis project follows strict Test-Driven Development practices using the Red-Green-Refactor cycle.\nv6 keeps red and green in a shared context so the predictions, error messages, and minimal\nimplementations stay coherent -- and isolates refactoring so the model evaluates the resulting\ncode on its own merits.\n\n## Architecture: Skills + Subagent\n\n- **`test-list`, `red`, `green`** -- Their SKILL.md files are auto-loaded as context.\n  Read each SKILL.md **before the first use** of that phase, then follow its instructions\n  directly in the main context. You execute the phase yourself, following the skill content.\n- **Refactor** -- Runs as a **Subagent with isolated context** via the `subagent` tool.\n  The refactor agent sees only the current source/tests, not the full red/green history.\n  Hypothesis: refactoring benefits most from a fresh perspective free of implementation bias.\n\nDo NOT skip reading the skill files. Do NOT skip the mandatory output markers.\n\n### Which Mechanism to Use:\n\n| Phase         | Mechanism                              | How to Execute                                              |\n|---------------|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Test List     | **Skill document** (main context)      | Read `.pi/skills/test-list/SKILL.md`, follow its steps      |\n| Red Phase     | **Skill document** (main context)      | Read `.pi/skills/red/SKILL.md`, follow its steps           |\n| Green Phase   | **Skill document** (main context)      | Read `.pi/skills/green/SKILL.md`, follow its steps         |\n| Refactor Phase| **Subagent** (isolated context)        | `subagent` tool with `agent: \"refactor\"`, `agentScope: \"both\"` |\n\n## TDD Workflow\n\n### 1. Test List Phase\n**READ SKILL**: `.pi/skills/test-list/SKILL.md`\n\nProvide: feature, test file path, implementation file path, requirements.\n\nThe skill creates a comprehensive test list using `it.todo()` covering every rule and example from the specification.\n\n**DO NOT** write the test list yourself. Follow the skill instructions and produce the `## Test List` marker.\n\n**DO NOT** end your turn after the test list. The moment the `## Test List`\nmarker is complete, continue in the same turn into the Red Phase for test 1\n(read `red/SKILL.md`, produce `## Red`). See \"One Continuous Autonomous Run\"\nat the top -- the Test-List -> Red boundary is where runs most often stall.\n\n### 2. Red Phase\n**READ SKILL**: `.pi/skills/red/SKILL.md`\n\nProvide: test file path, which `it.todo()` to activate, current passing-test count, implementation file path.\n\nThe skill activates exactly ONE test, makes explicit predictions, and verifies failure.\n\n**DO NOT** write test code yourself. Follow the skill instructions and produce the `## Red` and `Red Phase Complete:` markers with prediction lines.\n\n### 3. Green Phase\n**READ SKILL**: `.pi/skills/green/SKILL.md`\n\nProvide: test file path, failing test name, current error, implementation file path.\n\nThe skill implements minimal code to make the test pass -- hardcoded returns are fine for early tests.\n\n**DO NOT** write implementation code yourself. Follow the skill instructions and produce the `## Green` marker.\n\n### 4. Refactor Phase\n**INVOKE SUBAGENT**: Use the `subagent` tool with these parameters:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"agent\": \"refactor\",\n  \"agentScope\": \"both\",\n  \"task\": \"Test file: src/<feature>.spec.ts\\nImplementation file: src/<feature>.ts\\nPassing tests: <count>\\nRecent Green phase: <one-line summary of what was just added>\\n\\nRefactor the implementation while keeping all tests green.\\n\\nRun autonomously, return when done.\"\n}\n```\n\n`agentScope: \"both\"` is REQUIRED because the `refactor` agent lives in `.pi/agents/` (project-local). Without this flag the subagent extension only sees user-level agents and the call fails with \"Unknown agent: refactor\".\n\nThe agent will improve code while keeping tests green:\n- MUST attempt at least one refactoring (or document why none is possible)\n- Evaluate naming FIRST\n- Apply Four Rules of Simple Design (priority order)\n- Calculate APP (Absolute Priority Premise) mass before/after\n\n**DO NOT** refactor code yourself -- let the subagent do it. After it returns, read its summary, apply any test-runs needed for sanity, and proceed to the next Red phase.\n\n### 5. Repeat\nReturn to step 2 (Red phase) for the next test.\n\n## Core TDD Principles\n\n### TDD Mindset\nTDD practices will feel counterintuitive:\n- **Hardcoded returns feel \"too simple\"** -- This is correct!\n- **The urge to implement ahead is strong** -- Resist this\n- **Minimal steps feel inefficient** -- They actually accelerate development\n- **Predictions feel unnecessary** -- They build crucial understanding\n\n### Common TDD Failure Modes\n- **MISSING OUTPUT MARKERS** -- The most critical failure mode on pi.\n  Every cycle MUST have `## Red` and `## Green` headings.\n  Every Red cycle with a failing test MUST have `Red Phase Complete:` with prediction lines.\n- Multiple active tests at once\n- Implementing beyond what tests demand\n- Skipping predictions\n- Avoiding refactoring\n- Refactoring in the main context instead of via the subagent\n\n## Technical Setup: TypeScript and Vitest\n\n### TypeScript Conventions\n- Use proper type annotations\n- Leverage TypeScript's type checking during development\n- Name files after their content\n- Import with explicit `.js` extensions for local modules\n\n### Test File Conventions\n- Use `.spec.ts` extension for test files\n- Place tests near implementation files\n- Import test functions: `import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\"`\n\n### Running Tests\n\nRun tests with `pnpm test`.\n\n### Example Test Template\n\nThe test list comes from the kata's specification -- do not add generic \"validate input types\" or \"edge cases\" tests unless the spec calls for them.\n\n```typescript\n// some-feature.spec.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { someFeature } from \"./some-feature.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"Some Feature\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"[first behaviour from the spec]\");\n  it.todo(\"[second behaviour from the spec]\");\n});\n```\n\n## TDD Autonomous Execution\n\nThe TDD cycle runs autonomously -- no human-approval gates between phases.\nThe measurement pipeline parses the output text for phase markers\nand subagent tool calls; user prompts inserted between phases would\nsplit that sequence and produce unattributable cycles.\n\nWhen executing:\n- Do NOT wait for human approval between phases\n- Complete the full TDD cycle without interruption\n\n### Autonomous Workflow\n\n1. **Test List Phase** -- Read skill, produce `## Test List` marker\n2. **For each test:**\n   - **Red Phase** -- Read skill, produce `## Red` and `Red Phase Complete:` with predictions\n   - **Green Phase** -- Read skill, produce `## Green` marker\n   - **Refactor Phase** -- Invoke `subagent` tool with `agent: \"refactor\"`, `agentScope: \"both\"`\n3. **Continue** until all tests are implemented\n\n### Required Task Content for the Refactor Subagent\n\nThe refactor subagent has no memory of the red/green phases. Pass everything it needs in the `task` parameter:\n\n```\nTest file: [path]\nImplementation file: [path]\nPassing tests: [count]\nRecent changes: [one-line summary of the Green phase]\n\nRun autonomously, return after completion.\n```\n\nAfter the subagent returns, read its summary and proceed directly to the next Red phase.\n\n### Done Marker\n\nWhen all tests are implemented and passing, write a file `experiment-done.txt` with the single word `DONE` as its only content. Do not write any other summary or report file.\n\n## Remember\n\n- **NEVER STOP AT A PHASE BOUNDARY** -- Test List -> Red -> Green -> Refactor -> next Red all happen in one turn. Announcing the next phase is not doing it; take the action in the same turn. The only turn-end is after `experiment-done.txt` says `DONE`.\n- **ALWAYS PRODUCE THE MANDATORY MARKERS** -- `## Red`, `Red Phase Complete:`, `## Green`, `## Test List`\n- **ALWAYS USE THE SUBAGENT** for refactor -- `subagent` tool with `agent: \"refactor\"`, `agentScope: \"both\"`\n- Follow skill content (test-list/red/green SKILL.md) for the actual work\n- Pass `agentScope: \"both\"` to the `subagent` tool so the project-local refactor agent is discovered\n- Trust the process -- discomfort is a signal you're doing it right\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784963434636},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_G9vNoi0sd1klGTUCHyN2coUL","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_07-07-49_game-of-life-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_gpt-5-6-terra\n> vitest run\n\nsh: 1: vitest: not found\n ELIFECYCLE  Test failed. See above for more details.\n\n\nCommand exited with code 1"}],"details":{},"isError":true,"timestamp":1784963434636},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_N8KPcK1c1sjMqy2nMubi3cOY","name":"edit","arguments":{"path":"src/game-of-life.ts","edits":[{"oldText":"const cellKey = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;","newText":"const toCellKey = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;"},{"oldText":"cellKey(x, y)","newText":"toCellKey(x, y)"},{"oldText":"cellKey(x + deltaX, y + deltaY)","newText":"toCellKey(x + deltaX, y + deltaY)"},{"oldText":"return [...neighbourCounts].flatMap(([cellKey, count]) => {\n    const [x, y] = cellKey.split(\",\").map(Number);\n    return count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveCellKeys.has(cellKey)) ? [[x, y] as Cell] : [];\n  });","newText":"return [...neighbourCounts].flatMap(([key, count]) => {\n    const [x, y] = key.split(\",\").map(Number);\n    return count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveCellKeys.has(key)) ? [[x, y] as Cell] : [];\n  });"}]}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"azure/gpt-5.6-terra@swedencentral","usage":{"input":7529,"output":584,"cacheRead":5760,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":298,"totalTokens":13873,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784963434637,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-cf5f808a-8295-488b-a370-d17b82e9959d","responseModel":"gpt-5.6-terra-2026-07-09"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_N8KPcK1c1sjMqy2nMubi3cOY","toolName":"edit","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 4 block(s) in src/game-of-life.ts."}],"details":{"diff":"  1 export type Cell = [number, number];\n  2 \n- 3 const cellKey = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;\n+ 3 const toCellKey = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;\n  4 \n  5 export function nextGeneration(liveCells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n- 6   const liveCellKeys = new Set(liveCells.map(([x, y]) => cellKey(x, y)));\n+ 6   const liveCellKeys = new Set(liveCells.map(([x, y]) => toCellKey(x, y)));\n  7   const neighbourCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  8 \n  9   for (const [x, y] of liveCells) {\n 10     for (let deltaX = -1; deltaX <= 1; deltaX += 1) {\n 11       for (let deltaY = -1; deltaY <= 1; deltaY += 1) {\n 12         if (deltaX === 0 && deltaY === 0) continue;\n-13         const neighborKey = cellKey(x + deltaX, y + deltaY);\n+13         const neighborKey = toCellKey(x + deltaX, y + deltaY);\n 14         neighbourCounts.set(neighborKey, (neighbourCounts.get(neighborKey) ?? 0) + 1);\n 15       }\n 16     }\n 17   }\n 18 \n-19   return [...neighbourCounts].flatMap(([cellKey, count]) => {\n-20     const [x, y] = cellKey.split(\",\").map(Number);\n-21     return count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveCellKeys.has(cellKey)) ? [[x, y] as Cell] : [];\n+19   return [...neighbourCounts].flatMap(([key, count]) => {\n+20     const [x, y] = key.split(\",\").map(Number);\n+21     return count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveCellKeys.has(key)) ? [[x, y] as Cell] : [];\n 22   });\n 23 }","patch":"--- src/game-of-life.ts\n+++ src/game-of-life.ts\n@@ -1,23 +1,23 @@\n export type Cell = [number, number];\n \n-const cellKey = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;\n+const toCellKey = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;\n \n export function nextGeneration(liveCells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n-  const liveCellKeys = new Set(liveCells.map(([x, y]) => cellKey(x, y)));\n+  const liveCellKeys = new Set(liveCells.map(([x, y]) => toCellKey(x, y)));\n   const neighbourCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n \n   for (const [x, y] of liveCells) {\n     for (let deltaX = -1; deltaX <= 1; deltaX += 1) {\n       for (let deltaY = -1; deltaY <= 1; deltaY += 1) {\n         if (deltaX === 0 && deltaY === 0) continue;\n-        const neighborKey = cellKey(x + deltaX, y + deltaY);\n+        const neighborKey = toCellKey(x + deltaX, y + deltaY);\n         neighbourCounts.set(neighborKey, (neighbourCounts.get(neighborKey) ?? 0) + 1);\n       }\n     }\n   }\n \n-  return [...neighbourCounts].flatMap(([cellKey, count]) => {\n-    const [x, y] = cellKey.split(\",\").map(Number);\n-    return count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveCellKeys.has(cellKey)) ? [[x, y] as Cell] : [];\n+  return [...neighbourCounts].flatMap(([key, count]) => {\n+    const [x, y] = key.split(\",\").map(Number);\n+    return count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveCellKeys.has(key)) ? [[x, y] as Cell] : [];\n   });\n }\n","firstChangedLine":3},"isError":false,"timestamp":1784963440112},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_N52uFhXF2pPowT76cZ9yado9","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/game-of-life.ts"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_QmUs3z9zgvxKPTbaYbczaH2S","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pnpm test && pnpm exec tsc --noEmit","timeout":120}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_QC3bORUKwjEwDui1Cl7sWasw","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"git diff --check && git diff -- src/game-of-life.ts","timeout":120}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"azure/gpt-5.6-terra@swedencentral","usage":{"input":664,"output":145,"cacheRead":12928,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":43,"totalTokens":13737,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784963440113,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-9f6cd428-1244-4ac2-821c-1c19916b6c8e","responseModel":"gpt-5.6-terra-2026-07-09"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_N52uFhXF2pPowT76cZ9yado9","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"export type Cell = [number, number];\n\nconst toCellKey = (x: number, y: number): string => `${x},${y}`;\n\nexport function nextGeneration(liveCells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const liveCellKeys = new Set(liveCells.map(([x, y]) => toCellKey(x, y)));\n  const neighbourCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  for (const [x, y] of liveCells) {\n    for (let deltaX = -1; deltaX <= 1; deltaX += 1) {\n      for (let deltaY = -1; deltaY <= 1; deltaY += 1) {\n        if (deltaX === 0 && deltaY === 0) continue;\n        const neighborKey = toCellKey(x + deltaX, y + deltaY);\n        neighbourCounts.set(neighborKey, (neighbourCounts.get(neighborKey) ?? 0) + 1);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  return [...neighbourCounts].flatMap(([key, count]) => {\n    const [x, y] = key.split(\",\").map(Number);\n    return count === 3 || (count === 2 && liveCellKeys.has(key)) ? [[x, y] as Cell] : [];\n  });\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784963442621},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_QmUs3z9zgvxKPTbaYbczaH2S","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n> tdd-experiment-run@ test /home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_07-07-49_game-of-life-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_gpt-5-6-terra\n> vitest run\n\nsh: 1: vitest: not found\n ELIFECYCLE  Test failed. See above for more details.\n\n\nCommand exited with code 1"}],"details":{},"isError":true,"timestamp":1784963442621},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_QC3bORUKwjEwDui1Cl7sWasw","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"warning: Not a git repository. Use --no-index to compare two paths outside a working tree\nusage: git diff --no-index [<options>] <path> <path>\n\nDiff output format options\n    -p, --patch           generate patch\n    -s, --no-patch        suppress diff output\n    -u                    generate patch\n    -U, --unified[=<n>]   generate diffs with <n> lines context\n    -W, --function-context\n                          generate diffs with <n> lines context\n    --raw                 generate the diff in raw format\n    --patch-with-raw      syn